Material volumes, deformation maps and density (@fd-deformation-map: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J", #source "../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P", #fluid-dynamics "../mathematics/Differential-equations-boundary-data-and-a-solvable-flow-model.lingua#r_5HKW1NTEG39SPQQ5PF9THNHCMX") { r_1R8TG4FSEVRSMJRH25WAX6128Z
**Read:** [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J), chapters 3, 5 and 8 field descriptions and continuity; [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), kinematic transport theorem and fluid-motion notes.

A flow map sends an initial material label a to its current position x(a,t). Its deformation gradient describes changes in small material vectors. The determinant J gives the local volume ratio while the map remains regular. Conservation of material mass gives ρ(x,t)J=ρ₀(a).

Differentiate this account to connect density with expansion: DJ/Dt=J∇·u and Dρ/Dt=−ρ∇·u. Incompressible motion has ∇·u=0 and preserves material volume. It can still carry a spatially nonuniform density field whose value remains constant along each trajectory, if the other modelling assumptions allow it.

Apply the result to the planar stretching field and to uniform expansion. Identify when the map ceases to describe a single smooth material motion. This bridge connects a moving-particle description with the local continuity equation and explains what volume preservation actually means.

**Continue:** [Differential equations, boundary data and a solvable flow model](../mathematics/Differential-equations-boundary-data-and-a-solvable-flow-model.lingua#r_5HKW1NTEG39SPQQ5PF9THNHCMX).

